      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Lebanon ceasefire collapses, Hezbollah resumes mass fire, and the US-Iran Pakistani-channel collapses without a new signed framework before June 5 — driving resumed airstrikes and Hormuz re-closure hardening.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (base case):</strong> Talks grind through Pakistani mediation with no signed deal before June 5; Hormuz remains partially closed; Lebanon truce holds nominally with daily small-scale violations; Netanyahu coalition crisis managed but unresolved through late May.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Iran and US close nuclear enrichment gap in revised proposal, Witkoff-Araghchi meet directly, signed framework confirmed before June 5, Lloyd's premium drops, and Western shipping begins planned Hormuz return.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media gave FM Araghchi's "no trust" statement at the BRICS meeting substantial front-page treatment across IRNA, Press TV, and Tasnim between May 14-16. The framing was consistent: Iran is not refusing diplomacy but demanding proof of US seriousness, with Araghchi quoted directly as saying "We have every reason not to trust Americans, while they have no reason not to trust us." This framing presents Iran as the aggrieved but engaged party rather than the obstructor. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Tasnim and IRIB amplified parliament speaker Ghalibaf's May 12 "no alternative" declaration, framing the 14-point proposal as Iran's non-negotiable baseline. The state-media posture presents the proposal as generous and reasonable while casting US counter-demands — particularly on nuclear enrichment duration and Hormuz sovereignty — as colonial overreach. Kayhan editorials from the week of May 11-18 used language describing US negotiators as "dishonest brokers" without reaching explicit framework-rejection language per the monitored indicators. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Press TV's May 18 coverage of the revised Iranian proposal framed it as a "final and comprehensive" offer, with FM Spokesperson Baghaei quoted confirming delivery via Pakistan. The coverage avoided naming the nuclear enrichment gap as the primary obstacle, instead centering the narrative on Hormuz sovereignty and war-damage compensation — framing that positions Iran's public demands as legally grounded rather than strategic leverage. No Kayhan rejection-grade language ("rad," "mardud") appeared in relation to the US framework during the monitored window. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

